Reasoning: With 7 observations and a strong historical base rate of 15%, Bayesian shrinkage pulls the posterior substantially toward the prior. A weakly informative Beta prior centered at 15% yields a posterior mean around 33-40%, which is closer to 15% than to 85.7%. The small sample (n=7) is not enough to overcome the population prior with high confidence, but it does shift meaningfully from 15%. Confidence is moderate because: (a) n=7 is genuinely informative, (b) the prior strength assumption matters, and (c) the sample proportion is so extreme (6/7) that it can't be fully discounted.
